1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Los Angeles County sits on active faults.
In five years the USGS listed about two
thousand two hundred earthquakes of
magnitude two or more within a hundred
and sixty kilometres of a station like this.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:18,000
This twelve-dollar motion chip can feel
the stronger ones. A raw acceleration
number is not a magnitude. The chip has
no idea what it felt.

3
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The official list is public. Minutes after
every earthquake, the USGS publishes
magnitude, place and time. That list is
the catalog.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:36,000
So the station records a shake, then asks
the catalog whether an earthquake
happened at that second. A match is one
labelled example.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Enough matches and it fits a model for
this installation. That fit is what we
call calibration.

6
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The UNO Q is two computers. The
microcontroller reads the chip a hundred
times a second and runs STA/LTA: energy
in the last half-second over the last ten.

7
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The Linux side does WiFi, queries the
catalog, stores the record, and serves
the dashboard. They talk over the
Arduino Bridge.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
This is the station running on its own.
Every ten seconds the microcontroller
reports that it is alive.

9
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
I tap the box. Peak acceleration,
duration, frequency — measured live.

10
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
No match in the catalog: a tap is not an
earthquake. That is a detection of noise,
and the station says so.

11
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
On the second of September the catalog
published a magnitude three point two
near Ontario.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
The blind trigger never fired. It needed
about three times the amplitude that
arrived. The station keeps a continuous
envelope, went back, and read that second.

13
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The envelope sat four point three four
dispersions above the previous minutes.
We call that a confirmation: the ground
moved, and the catalog named the second.

14
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Without the catalog there was nothing to
look at. It is not a detection.

15
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
This part is a replay, not a live
recording. Real catalog times, synthetic
shaking, amplitudes deliberately about
thirty-eight times too large.

16
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
It shows the software: matching, fitting,
the dashboard. It does not show what the
sensor can feel.

17
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Red is the device’s estimate. Colour is
the catalog. The gap shrinks as the
amplitude model takes points.

18
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:32,000
The amplitude model needs eight matched
detections. The counter is still zero.
Confirmations are not allowed into that
fit.

19
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:42,000
The first autonomous catch has not
happened yet. In thirty days: nineteen
events examined, one confirmed, none that
should have been seen and were not.

20
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:50,000
It detects events that have already
happened. It does not predict. One
station gives a distance, a ring on a
map, not a pin.

21
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
A cheap sensor plus an official public
list: the box learns how the ground feels
here, and it keeps that model when the
internet is unplugged.
